      overview: "Islamic banks must match the financing mode to the customer's actual need and business type, and carefully align the tenor with the customer's cash flow since defaults cannot be remedied by charging extra for delay.",
      simpleExplanation: "There's no single Islamic loan product that fits every need - a bank has to pick the right tool (Murabaha for a one-off purchase, Ijarah for equipment, Salam for a farmer's future crop, and so on) for each customer's situation, and has to be extra careful about matching repayment timing to the customer's actual cash flow, because an Islamic bank can't just charge extra if someone pays late.",
      academicExplanation: "Deployment of funds is a major issue for IFIs since they lack conventional 'vanilla' products; using Murabaha or Ijarah for every need is neither feasible nor advisable (e.g. Murabaha is unsuitable for sugar cane purchase financing or long-term housing in high-inflation economies; Ijarah is unsuitable where asset/market/counterparty risk is high in long-term project financing). Diversification across modes gives better customer service and profit margins. Box 14.1 in the text summarises salient features: Murabaha (short/long-term, fixed rate, prepayment not allowed as a system, risk borne by financier then customer, late payment = loss to bank); Ijarah (long-term, fixed/variable rate, prepayment allowed, risk with financier, late payments controllable); Diminishing Musharakah (long-term, fixed/variable, prepayment allowed, joint risk, late payments controllable); Salam (short-term, fixed rate, no prepayment, risk with financier/customer, late payment = loss to bank); Istisna'a (short/long-term, fixed rate, may be structured for prepayment, risk customer/financier, late payments controllable); Musharakah/Mudarabah (short/long-term, variable rate, prepayment allowed, joint risk, no issue with late payments). A worked example (Box 14.2) shows Salam combined with Murabaha: a bank buys cotton from growers on Salam, takes a promise from a textile mill to purchase at an agreed price, appoints the mill as agent to take delivery, then sells to the mill under Murabaha - benefiting the farmer (advance cash, price-risk protection), the bank (extended deployment, diversified exposure, avoids Salam inventory problems) and the mill (hedge against cotton price fluctuation). On tenor: analysis of the customer's cash flow is critical for deciding financing tenor. Unlike conventional banking, where mismatched cash flows can sometimes be 'managed' through rollovers, this is 'suicidal' in Islamic finance because an Islamic bank cannot claim liquidated damages for loss of cost of funds on default - so tenor must be carefully determined in consultation with the customer from the outset.",
