      title: "Operational, Structural, Risk and Legal Controls in Financing",
      overview: "Beyond Shari'ah controls, Islamic banks need operational soundness checks, clear structuring, awareness of asymmetric-risk/moral-hazard issues, careful documentation and attention to the legal framework.",
      simpleExplanation: "Running an Islamic bank safely also means checking that its feasibility studies and project monitoring are solid, that even a tiny slip in a transaction's structure doesn't accidentally send the whole profit to charity, that staff understand risks like a client walking away from an Ijarah asset, and that all the paperwork and legal groundwork is in place before money moves.",
      academicExplanation: "Because Islamic banks' assets are risk-based (equity participation and profit-sharing as well as debt-based trade/leasing modes), regulators need operational soundness procedures beyond mere debtor-solvency checks: (1) application of consolidated, acceptable accounting standards suited to Islamic modes; (2) review of project financing operations for soundness of feasibility studies, evaluations and implementation follow-up; (3) evaluation of the bank's performance in monitoring/controlling equity-financed enterprises, including its ability to address problems and provide technical assistance. Structural issues: every aspect of a transaction's structure should be clarified in advance by the Shari'ah board, since a slight process-flow change can divert the whole income to charity for non-compliance; IFIs dealing in goods/tangible assets may need to adapt their business structure accordingly. Asymmetric risk and moral hazard: asymmetric risk arises from information asymmetry (one party has better information than the other); moral hazard is increased risk of problematic behaviour because the person causing the problem doesn't bear its full consequences (a concern especially relevant to Takaful on financed assets). Bankers must understand rights/liabilities under each mode - e.g. in Ijarah, a client may stop using the asset, ask the bank to take it back and refuse to purchase it despite an earlier promise; Ijarah principles allow this, so banks must plan mitigation (e.g. reviewing client payment history) from the outset. Documentation: proper preparation and timely execution of documents for each contract can save the bank from unforeseen losses - e.g. an agency agreement executed at L/C opening (rather than left informal) lets the bank undertake Murabaha to cover its cost of funds if the client later doesn't pay; sequencing and timing of documents in the master facility agreement is critical, requiring trained staff. Legal framework: Islamic banking products may need re-engineering to conform to the prevailing legal framework (which may differ from conventional banking's), often requiring regulators' guidance; since Islamic banks cannot recover liquidated damages for late payment through contract, they must understand available legal options and third-party obligation/guarantee issues in asset-based trade and leasing business.",
