      simpleExplanation: "A business often needs more than just money to buy raw materials - it also needs to cover wages and overheads while making the goods, and Islamic banks meet this by stacking several modes together, like using Murabaha for materials and Istisna'a to fund the manufacturing itself.",
      academicExplanation: "Murabaha is the most popular mode for working capital, financing the purchase of raw materials, but Murabaha alone cannot fund labour and overhead expenses, particularly for exporters. Combining Murabaha (for raw material purchase) with Istisna'a (to manufacture goods and pay overheads) and Wakalah (customer as agent) allows a fuller solution: the customer manufactures and delivers goods per the L/C, which then become the bank's property; the exporter, as the bank's agent, exports the goods, and export proceeds are remitted to the bank, which deducts the Istisna'a price and profit before the client pays the Murabaha price on schedule. Musharakah can also be used for working capital via the 'daily product' concept; bank and client may agree to share gross profits (rather than net), meaning the client voluntarily bears indirect expenses like depreciation and administrative salaries - a factor that should influence the sharing ratio (a larger share to the client) - while direct expenses (raw materials, direct labour, electricity) are borne jointly. Salam can effectively finance working capital in sectors like sugar, fertilizer and cement. For a sugar mill: (1) the bank enters a Salam agreement, paying the full price in advance for sugar to be delivered later, and may take a charge on the mill's assets as security; (2) the bank appoints the mill as its agent to sell the sugar in the market at an agreed/advised price, with any excess over that price allowed to the mill as a bonus if so provided; (3) on the delivery date, the mill informs the bank, which takes delivery and authorizes the mill to sell on its behalf; (4) the mill sells and pays the price to the bank - if higher than agreed, the bank may pay the mill the excess (if promised); if lower, the bank bears the loss.",
      examExplanation: "Working capital procedure: Murabaha (raw material) + Istisna'a (manufacture, pay overheads) + Wakalah (agent exports) = full working capital cycle. Musharakah via daily product with gross-profit sharing lets client absorb indirect costs. Salam working capital: advance payment -> agency to sell -> delivery -> settlement (bank bears downside if market price falls below agreed price).",
