      academicExplanation: "Trade finance conversion is relatively easier since it usually involves assets. Musharakah/Mudarabah use in trade finance remains minimal but has growth potential on consignment or single-transaction bases. Banks charge service fees for opening L/Cs, fund imports via PLS or Murabaha, and charge fees as negotiating banks for exports, providing preshipment export financing via PLS or Murabaha. Post-shipment bill discounting, being Riba-based, must be replaced: banks provide interest-free loans against bills and take them over for collection, charging an amount-related (not time-related) service fee as collecting agent - applicable to inland and export bills, and any Shari'ah-compliant mode (Musharakah, Mudarabah, Istisna'a) may be used. A worked example: an exporter with a $100,000 bill realizable in 55 days approaches the bank; at a spot Dollar rate of Rs. 57.75, the bank quotes Rs. 57.60/US$ and structures a Musawamah-based transaction - disbursing funds under an agency agreement for raw material purchase, the client then offers the purchased stock for sale to the bank at US$100,000 payable on the due date (accepted, concluding a sale); on the due date, the foreign bank remits US$100,000 to the client's account and the Islamic bank debits it per the client's authority. Commodity operations: Islamic banks can conduct government commodity operations (e.g. wheat procurement) via Murabaha, forming a syndicate/company to buy from farmers/suppliers and sell to provincial governments with a profit margin, using existing trade entities as agents. Murabaha-Istisna'a export financing helps textile/composite exporters needing liquidity for labour/overheads beyond raw material purchase, combining Murabaha with Istisna'a and Wakalah; Istisna'a is a permitted exception to full-prepayment-required forward sale rules, requiring only a fixed price and clear specifications. Import financing through Murabaha: bank and customer sign a master Murabaha and agency agreement; the customer purchases from foreign suppliers as the bank's agent; settlement can occur via (a) normal payment (Musawamah declaration, cost + charges, or a sub-Murabaha if financing is needed, profit charged from Nostro-debit date to settlement date); (b) payment against documents (PAD); (c) trust receipt (TR) Murabaha for longer financing periods (120+ days); (d) shipping guarantees/delivery orders, where a margin (e.g. 110%) is taken and a sub-Murabaha executed at the prevailing FX rate, with price differences settled afterward on the cost portion only (not the profit portion, since Murabaha profit, once fixed, cannot be adjusted).",
