      academicExplanation: "Ijarah is currently the most popular project finance mode, but cannot be used effectively where a project needs to be installed or constructed - requiring alternatives. For cement plant construction: the bank provides Musharakah-basis finance and enters an Istisna'a contract with the industrial concern, appointing the customer as agent to supervise erection; the bank then enters a Musharakah contract with the client to operate the plant, earning periodic profit as rental on its ownership share, with principal recovered by selling units to the client at market price (via proper offer/acceptance) either progressively or in one transfer at investment end; partnership may also simply share profits once production begins. Syndication for an oil terminal: a financing consortium and terminal operator agree to an Ijarah (a unilateral binding promise); an Istisna'a agreement is executed between a construction contractor and the consortium; the consortium appoints the terminal operator as agent to supervise construction; once ready, the consortium and operator enter the actual Ijarah agreement. Syndication for Sukuk issue and securitization: banks may syndicate to finance public-sector and large corporate projects through securitization/Sukuk; the securities represent proportionate Sukuk-holder ownership of underlying assets. Per the majority of Islamic schools, the securitized asset pool should comprise Ijarah or fixed assets worth more than 50% of total pool value for the Sukuk to be tradable at any secondary-market value (the Hanafi view permits trading even where illiquid assets exceed only 10% of total worth). Investors in the pool have a Musharakah relationship and are proportionate owners; Sukuk holders assume the pool's rights/obligations to the extent of their ownership, though if the pool contains debt instruments (e.g. Murabaha receivables), the Sukuk price cannot be less than the value of those debt instruments.",
