      title: "Liquidity Management Tools for Islamic Banks",
      overview: "Islamic banks manage liquidity through inter-bank Mudarabah placements, Sukuk sale/purchase, asset-pool investment, Parallel Salam and (cautiously) Tawarruq.",
      simpleExplanation: "When an Islamic bank has extra cash or needs cash quickly, it has several Shari'ah-compliant tools available - lending to other banks on a profit-sharing basis, buying or selling Sukuk in the market, pooling its Murabaha/Ijarah assets for other banks to invest in, or as a last resort using Tawarruq, though scholars warn this should be used sparingly.",
      academicExplanation: "Direct placement of funds in the open market by surplus banks for deficit banks uses the Mudarabah contract, with a deficit bank agreeing to share general profits per a negotiable Mudarabah ratio; central banks may issue guiding principles. Liquidity management via securitization: an Islamic bank purchases Sukuk from the government at par at primary issue, earning rental/profit; if it needs liquidity, it sells the Sukuk in the secondary market (to another bank, or to the central bank if the market is illiquid) as an outright sale, and if in surplus, purchases Sukuk from the market - similar in effect to conventional repo/reverse-repo but structured as two separate outright transactions rather than a repurchase agreement. Alternatively, without selling/purchasing Sukuk, a bank can create an asset pool of its own Murabaha and Ijarah assets and invite other banks/NBFCs to invest (with Murabaha receivables kept below 50% of pool assets); at maturity, the investing bank redeems its investment and the originating bank pays its profit share. Parallel Salam contracts, where the purchaser pays the full price in advance, can also meet liquidity needs. Tawarruq is used to both place and obtain liquidity: an Islamic bank needing funds and a surplus bank select a liquid commodity (e.g. Commodity Exchange metals or blue-chip stocks); the bank in need purchases the commodity on credit (Murabaha) from the other party, takes delivery, then sells it in the market at spot price (the process reverses for placing liquidity). While seemingly simple, extreme care is needed to ensure the transaction is not a mere exchange of papers. Tawarruq gives a fixed guaranteed return on the asset side and can even be executed with conventional banks; Malaysian Islamic bank credit cards use this concept combined with buy-back, though the majority of Shari'ah scholars consider such cards non-Shari'ah-compliant. 'Commodity Murabaha' (Tawarruq-based) is used by a number of Middle Eastern IFIs but is considered a grey area, to be used only in extreme cases to avoid interest where no other option exists, under Shari'ah board guidance - its widespread use is considered harmful to the industry long-term.",
      examExplanation: "Liquidity tools: (1) inter-bank Mudarabah placement, (2) Sukuk sale/purchase (two separate outright transactions, similar effect to repo but not a repurchase agreement), (3) Murabaha/Ijarah asset pool investment by other banks (Murabaha receivables <50%), (4) Parallel Salam, (5) Tawarruq (grey area - scholars warn against widespread use).",
