      academicExplanation: "Four card types exist: debit (immediate debit from available funds, no credit provided), charge (credit facility to a ceiling with interest only on late payment beyond the free period, no revolving facility), credit (revolving credit facility, interest normally charged on carried balances) and ATM cards. The OIC Fiqh Council (12th session, 2000) resolved: issuing/using a credit card with an interest condition is impermissible even if the holder intends to pay within the interest-free moratorium; but issuing credit cards without any interest condition is permissible, with the issuer entitled to a specific issuance/renewal fee (any charge beyond this is impermissible as usurious) and a merchant commission (provided cash/credit prices to the merchant's customers are equal). This implies Islamic 'credit cards' are structurally charge cards: annual/recurring fees can be recovered from cardholders, and transaction commissions from merchants, but no return may be charged on any loan/debt created. Debit cards are permissible without interest as long as the balance is not exceeded. Charge cards are permissible subject to: no interest obligation for late payment; any required guarantee deposit must be invested for the cardholder's benefit on a Mudarabah basis if unavailable for the holder's use; the card must not be usable for Shari'ah-prohibited purposes; gold/silver/currency purchase is allowed via debit/charge card where the issuing bank can settle without a credit period. Possible features of a Shari'ah-compliant credit card: annual issuance fee, interest-free revolving credit line, purchase rights up to a limit, emergency cash limit, merchant commission, monthly minimum principal repayment with the remainder deferred interest-free, a charity-directed default penalty, and prohibition on use for Shari'ah-prohibited purposes. Real-world structures discussed: Emirates Islamic Bank's card (Ujrah/service-charge based, quarterly fixed fee, minimum 10% monthly payment, no interest); Bank Islam Malaysia Card (BIC) (based on Bai' al 'Inah - buy-back of land at a cash price then repurchase at a higher deferred price - plus Wadi'ah and Qard al Hasan; its Shari'ah position is questionable since most scholars outside the Far East reject Bai' al 'Inah); AmBank Al-Taslif Card (also Bai' al 'Inah-based, cash sale + deferred purchase contract, avoiding six prohibited merchant categories); Kuwait Finance House Al Tayseer Card (annual fee, one-third monthly minimum repayment, no interest on the revolving two-thirds, dual Visa/MasterCard on one account); Kuwait Finance House (Bahrain) Ijarah Card (Ijarah-based financing of durable goods up to 25 months). Other possible structures: Murabaha-based cards (bank buys goods from the store, sells on deferred payment to the customer, merchant as agent, master Murabaha contract, choice of tenure/profit-rate packages) and Musharakah-based cards (bank funds participating stores on profit-sharing, issuing cards for customer purchases at those stores).",
