      academicExplanation: "Islamic banks cannot operate totally isolated from conventional banks; cooperation is needed in corresponding services, foreign trade financing, project co-financing, and temporary (non-interest) fund placement to ensure liquidity, along with information exchange and joint training programmes. On fee-based services: Underwriting - the underwriter binds itself to procure the underwritten capital for a fee/commission, but take-up commission for subscribing to unsubscribed shares is not permissible; shares subscribed by the underwriter must be at the offer price without adjustment. The OIC Fiqh Council (7th session, 1992) resolved there is no Shari'ah objection to underwriting provided the underwriter subscribes at nominal value without compensation for the commitment itself, though compensation for other services (e.g. studies, marketing) is permitted. Letters of Guarantee (L/G) - jurists generally disallow fees purely for guarantees, but some permit banks to recover administrative-expense-related fees/commission since a guarantee bundles services including Wakalah; if the guarantee is called, the bank may recover only its principal amount. Letters of Credit (L/C) - scholars differ: some treat L/Cs as a fixed-rate service (not varying with duration/volume); others allow fee variation with volume (reflecting varying staff effort); some use bracket-based rather than percentage-based rate structures; others treat L/Cs as agency arrangements at a fixed percentage. The general/suggested view is to treat L/Cs as a banking service (not a guarantee, except standby L/Cs), with the fee reflecting effort exerted (varying by L/C type - partial shipment, revolving, confirmed, red clause) rather than time. L/Cs may be opened on Murabaha basis (bank opens for itself/via agency, sells to customer FOB/CIF on possession, fee added to total cost) or Musharakah basis (more flexible - L/C in customer's or bank's name, either party can administer, partner may buy the bank's share or the Musharakah is liquidated on receipt of goods). Islamic banks may open documentary credits at conventional banks and ask correspondents to confirm L/Cs for foreign suppliers, keeping surplus balances to cover obligations; care is needed to avoid delaying value transfer (risking correspondent interest charges) and to avoid interest-based supplier payment facilities. Reciprocal arrangements allow Islamic banks limited overdraft facilities from correspondents without interest in exchange for maintaining reasonable current-account balances and prompt debit coverage; correspondent banks debit a 'cash margin' as partial security. Transfer of funds in the same currency is allowed with or without a fee (echoing the traditional Suftajah instrument); cross-currency transfers require the exchange operation to be carried out at the agreed rate before transfer.",
